---
import Adr from "../../components/Adr.astro";
import Inferred from "../../components/Inferred.astro";
import Layer from "../../components/Layer.astro";
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import PageLink from "../../components/PageLink.astro";
import Path from "../../components/Path.astro";
import Routes from "../../components/Routes.astro";
import Rule from "../../components/Rule.astro";
import Sequence from "../../components/Sequence.astro";

const S = "server/src/iki_server/entities/services.py";
const C = "server/src/iki_server/entities/checking.py";
const H = "server/src/iki_server/entities/holds.py";
const API = "server/src/iki_server/api/entities.py";

const toc = [
  { id: "short", label: "Everything is checked, then written in one transaction" },
  { id: "how", label: "Creating and editing" },
  { id: "save", label: "One save, step by step" },
  { id: "rules", label: "Rules, holds and what the database holds" },
  { id: "ref", label: "Routes, files and decisions" },
];
---

<Layout toc={toc}>
  <Layer n={1} title="Everything is checked, then written in one transaction">
    <p>
      Every write of a record's values goes through one service, whether a person, an agent, a
      run's results, a bulk import or setup data makes it. A create checks the type, each value and
      the plugin's rules first, then writes the row, revision 1, its history and its entry in one
      transaction. An edit does the same under the record's row lock, for the revision the client
      read. A rule that's broken refuses the save, or, for a rule that holds, keeps it only with a
      reason and holds the record for review. <code>unique</code> and <code>no_overlap</code>{" "}
      rules are held by the database (<Adr n={[35, 127]} />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={2} title="Creating and editing">
    <p>
      <code>POST /api/v1/entities</code> needs edit access to the project. The service checks
      everything first (<Path p={S} sym="def prepare(" />), then writes in one transaction. <code
        >PATCH /api/v1/entities/{"{ref}"}</code
      > sends only the values to change and the ETag the client read (<PageLink
        to="Revisions and history"
      /> covers the ETag). Fields it doesn't name keep their values. On an edit, only the rules
      reading a field it changes run, as Kubernetes ratchets its validation: a record saved before a
      rule existed can still change in other ways, and a deploy reports it rather than leaving it
      to fail later.
    </p>
    <p>
      An edit refuses, field by field: a field the type hasn't got; a <code>computed</code> field
      unless a run writes it; the amount a ledger records; a state field, which is set on its own
      (<PageLink to="States and guards" />); an identity field once set; an <code>immutable</code>{" "}
      field once set; a required field cleared. A locked record refuses every value edit, unless the
      edit is a correction (<PageLink to="Locks and corrections" />). A Boolean marked <code
        >reason_on_clear</code
      > needs a reason to clear (<Path p={S} sym="def update(" />).
    </p>

    <h3 id="save">One save, step by step</h3>
    <p>
      What <code>POST /api/v1/entities</code> does. All of it after the first phase is one database
      transaction; CHECK constraints and the lock and unique triggers fire as the rows are written.
    </p>
    <Sequence
      id="save-seq"
      title="Creating a record"
      width={900}
      actors={[
        { id: "client", label: "Client", sub: "app, SDK or agent" },
        { id: "api", label: "API", sub: "api/entities.py" },
        { id: "svc", label: "Services", sub: "entities/services.py" },
        { id: "wasm", label: "Rules", sub: "iki-wasm (CEL)" },
        { id: "db", label: "Postgres" },
      ]}
      steps={[
        { phase: "Checked first, outside the transaction" },
        { id: "post", from: "client", to: "api", label: "POST /api/v1/entities" },
        { id: "call", from: "api", to: "svc", label: "create()" },
        { id: "prepare", from: "svc", to: "svc", label: "prepare: type, each value, defaults, required" },
        { id: "cel", from: "svc", to: "wasm", label: "plugin rules on the new values" },
        { id: "broken", from: "wasm", to: "svc", label: "breaks: refuse, or hold with a reason", dashed: true },
        { phase: "One transaction" },
        { id: "entry", from: "svc", to: "db", label: "find or make the entry, under an advisory lock" },
        { id: "row", from: "svc", to: "db", label: "insert the typed row: HRID, identity hash" },
        { id: "revise", from: "svc", to: "db", label: "revision 1, then queue webhooks and triggers" },
        { id: "history", from: "svc", to: "db", label: "history rows, file uses, project link" },
        { id: "after", from: "svc", to: "db", label: "notifications; the hold; the amount" },
        { id: "reply", from: "api", to: "client", label: "201, or 200 reused · ETag: the revision", dashed: true },
      ]}
    >
      <div slot="post">
        <p>Needs edit access to the target project. The MCP tool <code>create_entity</code> calls the same endpoint.</p>
        <p><Path p={API} sym="def create_entity(" />, <Path p="server/src/iki_server/mcp/tools.py" sym="create_entity" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="call">
        <p>Run results, imports, collections and setup data create records through the same service, so every path gets the same checks.</p>
        <p><Path p={S} sym="def create(" />, <Path p="server/src/iki_server/runs/results.py" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="prepare">
        <p>
          Refuses an abstract or frozen type; an unknown, removed, ledger, computed (unless a run
          writes it) or state field; converts and validates each value. New records start each state
          field at its <code>initial</code>. Then the core types' own rules in Python.
        </p>
        <p><Path p={S} sym="def prepare(" />, <Path p="server/src/iki_server/entities/rules.py" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="cel">
        <p>The record's values go to iki-wasm under wasmtime, with a fuel budget per call; the browser runs the same module (<Adr n="0127" />). <PageLink to="WebAssembly" /> covers the module.</p>
        <p><Path p={C} sym="def broken(" />, <Path p="spec/src/iki_spec/wasm.py" sym="FUEL" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="broken">
        <p>
          <code>raise_broken</code> refuses with every broken rule named, or, for rules that hold
          rather than refuse, needs a hold reason (422 <code>hold_reason_required</code> without
          one). <code>apart</code> names the record a unique or no-overlap rule clashes with.
        </p>
        <p><Path p={C} sym="def raise_broken(" />, <Path p={C} sym="def apart(" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="entry">
        <p>Inside the transaction a result for a request is checked against it, under its lock, and a project starter's flow may refuse the type. Then the entry: the lock key is the registry and the identity's digest, so two creates of the same thing can't both make one (<PageLink to="Identity and HRIDs" />).</p>
        <p><Path p={S} sym="iki_entry:" />, <Path p="server/src/iki_server/entities/identity.py" sym="def hash_for(" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="row">
        <p><code>Entity.save</code> sets the type, takes the next HRID (or the entry's) and, for an entry, the identity hash. Extension and list rows follow.</p>
        <p><Path p={S} sym="def _new_record(" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="revise">
        <p>
          <code>_revise</code> inserts the <code>Revision</code> and bumps <code>Entity.revision</code>;{" "}
          <code>_announce</code> then queues webhooks and records trigger firings in the same
          transaction, so they exist exactly when the change does (<Adr n={[31, 80]} />).
        </p>
        <p><Path p={S} sym="def _revise(" />, <Path p={S} sym="def _announce(" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="history">
        <p>A full row per touched table into its <code>__history</code>, the files the record uses, and its owner project's link.</p>
        <p><Path p={S} sym="def _write_history(" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="after">
        <p>Assignment notices and the type's declared notifications (<Adr n="0129" />); if a rule held it, the hold and its <code>entity.held</code> audit event; for a type with an amount, its first ledger movement (<PageLink to="Amounts and the ledger" />).</p>
        <p><Path p="server/src/iki_server/notifications/declared.py" />, <Path p={H} sym="def hold(" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="reply">
        <p>201 for a new record; 200 with <code>reused</code> when the project already had a record of that identity; <code>registered</code> says whether a new entry was made.</p>
        <p><Path p={API} sym="def create_entity(" /></p>
      </div>
    </Sequence>
    <p>
      <Inferred why="The code only queues rows in the transaction; the delivery workers weren't traced.">
        Webhook delivery and the runs triggers start happen after commit, from the task runner and
        the trigger machinery; this page shows only that they're queued in the transaction.
      </Inferred>
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={4} title="Rules, holds and what the database holds">
    <div class="rules">
      <Rule title="CEL rules" open>
        <p>
          A type's rules are its own, its ancestors' and those its extensions declare. A <code
            >check</code
          > is CEL over the record as it will be saved, type-checked when the plugin is validated
          and evaluated by iki-wasm on the server and in browsers' forms alike. <code>when</code>{" "}
          limits it to records it's true of; <code>fields</code> names the fields a failure points at
          in forms and errors. An edit runs the checks that read a field it changes (or that read
          none). A check that can't be worked out because a value it reads is
          empty passes, as SQL's CHECK does with NULL; one that can't be worked out otherwise breaks
          (<Path p={C} />, <Path p="spec/src/iki_spec/rules.py" />).
        </p>
        <p>
          <code>reference_revisions</code> makes a check read the records a reference names at the
          revisions this record pinned, not as they are now; <code>recheck_before_signing</code>{" "}
          runs it again at each signed state change (<Path
            p="spec/src/iki_spec/schemas/rule/v1.json"
          />). The <PageLink to="States and guards" hash="example" /> worked example uses both.
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Holds: a broken specification is recorded, not refused">
        <p>
          A rule with <code>hold</code> is a specification, such as a result's limits. A result
          outside it is data, and refusing it invites testing into compliance, so it's kept, with a
          reason, and the record is held. A hold is cleared by a review: a person (not an agent or a
          service account) with one of the rule's lab roles in the record's project, and not whoever
          saved it, says why it may stand. Editing it back within the rule keeps the hold; saving it
          outside again adds to it. While any hold is open the record can't enter a state that locks
          it (<Adr n="0127" />, <Path p={H} sym="def blocks_locking(" />). A run's or an import's
          results hold with a reason naming the run. Searches find what rules caught (<Adr
            n="0135"
          />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="What the database holds" id="database">
        <p>
          <code>unique</code> fields (<Adr n="0082" />) and the <code>unique</code> and <code
            >no_overlap</code
          > rules are triggers that keep a row per record in a side table: <code
            >iki_unique_value</code
          > for a combination, <code>iki_range_value</code> for a range, whose exclusion constraint
          refuses two overlapping ones in a project. So a person, a run, a bulk import and SQL are
          all held to it, and two saves at once can't both get in. A rule's <code>while</code> counts
          only records whose enum, state or Boolean fields have one of its values: a tube that's
          stored, a booking that isn't cancelled. Before writing, <code>taken</code> finds the
          record a save would clash with, so the person is told which (<Path
            p="server/src/iki_server/entities/constraints.py"
          />, <Path p="server/src/iki_server/entities/unique.py" />).
        </p>
        <p>
          A unique registry fact (a ChEMBL ID) is held once per registry instead. Projects are kept
          apart: who holds a clashing value is told only to someone who can read it.
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="When a plugin's rules change">
        <p>
          A deploy that adds or changes a rule fills in the side tables for records already there,
          and stops, naming them, if records already break a <code>unique</code> or <code
            >no_overlap</code
          > rule. Each type whose checks changed has its records checked again: those breaking a
          rule are reported, or for a rule that holds, held by the system (<Path
            p="server/src/iki_server/entities/breaches.py"
          />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
    </div>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={5} title="Routes, files and decisions">
    <Routes paths={/^\/api\/v1\/entities(\/\{ref\})?(\/holds[^]*)?$/} />
    <div class="refs">
      <div>
        <h3>Where</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><Path p={S} />: create, update, archive</li>
          <li><Path p={C} />: plugins' rules</li>
          <li><Path p={H} />: holds and their review</li>
          <li><Path p="server/src/iki_server/entities/constraints.py" /></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Decisions</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><Adr n="0035" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0082" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0127" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0135" title /></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Layer>
</Layout>
